---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EventList from '../../components/EventList.astro';
import { getUpcomingEvents } from '../../lib/calendar.js';

const upcoming = (await getUpcomingEvents()).slice(0, 3);
---

<Base
  title="How to make friends in Kansas City"
  description="A practical guide to meeting people in Kansas City: where to start, what actually works, and how to turn a first conversation into a real friendship."
  path="/guides/how-to-make-friends-in-kansas-city"
>
  <section class="section section--white">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <h1>How to make friends in Kansas City</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Moving here, coming out of a long stretch of work, or just realizing your circle got small.
        Here's what actually works for meeting people in this city.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--white">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow prose">
      <h2>Pick something that repeats</h2>
      <p>
        One-off events are hard to build on. You meet someone, have a good conversation, and never
        see them again. Recurring gatherings work better because friendship comes from repeated
        low-stakes contact, not from a single great first meeting. A weekly walk, a monthly game
        night, a class that runs eight weeks. The repetition does the work.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the single biggest difference between people who build a circle here and people who
        stay stuck. Choose the thing that happens again next week.
      </p>

      <h2>Choose activities where talking is the point</h2>
      <p>
        A movie night puts everyone in a dark room facing forward. A walk puts you shoulder to
        shoulder with nothing to do but talk, which is why walking groups tend to produce actual
        friendships faster than most other activities. Side-by-side conversation is easier than
        face-to-face for most people, and there's a natural exit when the walk ends.
      </p>
      <p>Other formats that work the same way: group runs, volunteer shifts, rec league sports,
        book clubs, and anything with a shared task in the middle.</p>

      <h2>Where to look in Kansas City</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Walking and running groups.</strong> Free, no commitment, and they meet on a
          fixed schedule. Most KC groups welcome people who show up alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Parks and trails.</strong> Loose Park, Tomahawk Creek Trail, and the trail system
          across the metro host a steady stream of organized group activity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Libraries and community centers.</strong> Kansas City Public Library branches run
          free programming, including book clubs, that nobody thinks to check.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Volunteering.</strong> Shared work builds rapport quickly, and shifts repeat.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rec leagues and classes.</strong> Kickball, volleyball, pottery, language groups.
          The activity gives you something to talk about immediately.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Go alone</h2>
      <p>
        Bringing a friend feels safer and it works against you. If you arrive with someone, you'll
        talk to that person and leave having met nobody. Showing up alone is uncomfortable for
        about six minutes and then it isn't. Most groups have people there for exactly the same
        reason you are.
      </p>

      <h2>Go back a second time</h2>
      <p>
        The first time is introductions. The second time is when people remember your name, and the
        third is when you start getting invited to things. Most people quit after one visit because
        it felt awkward, which is the normal way a first visit feels. The awkwardness is not a
        signal to stop.
      </p>

      <h2>Make the small ask</h2>
      <p>
        At some point somebody has to suggest coffee after, or a different event next week. It
        might as well be you. Keep it small and specific, tied to something already happening, and
        easy to say no to. Low stakes on both sides.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--cream">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>An easy place to start</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Our walks are free, they repeat, and most people come alone. No sign-up, no dues. Show up at
        the meeting spot and walk with us.
      </p>
      <EventList
        events={upcoming}
        emptyMessage="Nothing on the calendar right this minute. New dates go up regularly."
      />
      <p style="margin-top:2rem"><a class="btn" href="/what-we-do#walks">How the walks work</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
